{{Japanese name|Sadanuma}}

'''Koichiro Sadanuma''' (定沼 耕一郎, ''Sadanuma Kōichirō''; born 10 August 1981) is a Sanese politician who has served as [[Leader of the Opposition (Okaiken)|Leader of the Opposition]] since October 2025. He has been the President of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) since 2023 and a member of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] representing [[Akita 2nd District]] since 2012.

A former Ministry of Finance bureaucrat, Sadanuma is widely credited with revitalizing the LDP following its decline in the early 2020s. Positioning himself as a fiscal conservative and a moderate pragmatist, he led the party to a significant resurgence in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], gaining 40 seats and displacing the [[Sanese Communist Party]] as the primary opposition to the [[Honda Shigetsuma]] administration.

He is known for his stoic demeanor, policy-driven leadership style, and his close political partnership with Shadow Foreign Minister [[Reo Nakasone]].

==Early life and education==
Sadanuma was born in [[Akita (city)|Akita City]], [[Akita Prefecture]], in 1981. Unlike many prominent LDP figures, he does not come from a hereditary political dynasty; his father was a rice farmer and his mother was a schoolteacher.

He attended local public schools before gaining admission to the prestigious [[University of Tokyo]], where he studied Economics. Upon graduation in 2004, he entered the [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]] (MOF). During his time as a bureaucrat, Sadanuma specialized in budget allocation and tax policy, gaining a reputation as a "fiscal hawk" deeply concerned with Okaiken's national debt. He resigned from the ministry in 2012 to enter politics.

==Political career==
===Early Diet Career===
Sadanuma was first elected to the House of Commoners in the 2012 general election, winning the Akita 2nd District seat. He joined the LDP's moderate faction (Kōchikai), focusing his legislative efforts on rural revitalization and administrative reform.

During the party's turbulent years in opposition, Sadanuma rose through the Policy Research Council, becoming known as one of the party's sharpest policy minds. He was a vocal critic of the "reckless nationalism" promoted by the faction led by [[Hakuya Terakubo]], arguing that the LDP needed to return to competence rather than culture wars to win back voters.

===LDP President (2023–Present)===
Following the resignation of the previous party leadership in late 2022, the LDP held a presidential election on 12 February 2023. The contest was viewed as a battle for the soul of the party between the nationalist right-wing and the moderate technocrats. Sadanuma defeated the nationalist candidate, Hakuya Terakubo, by a narrow margin, becoming one of the youngest leaders in the party's history at age 41.

Upon taking office, Sadanuma appointed a "Shadow Cabinet of Experts," including bringing in veteran diplomat [[Reo Nakasone]] as Shadow Minister of Foreign Affairs. He focused the party's messaging on "Fiscal Sanity" and "Responsible Governance," directly targeting the spending policies of the PPO government.

===2025 General Election===
Sadanuma led the LDP into the 2025 general election with the slogan "Competence Returns." He relentlessly attacked Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] over the costs of the [[National Universal Health Act]] and the resulting tax hikes.

Although his campaign faced a setback when he delayed condemning xenophobic remarks made by rival faction member Hakuya Terakubo, Sadanuma stabilized the ship by focusing on economic data. The election resulted in the LDP winning 128 seats, a gain of 40 seats from the previous election. This victory restored the LDP as the second-largest party in the Diet, allowing Sadanuma to assume the constitutional role of Leader of the Opposition.

==Leader of the Opposition==
As Leader of the Opposition, Sadanuma has positioned the LDP as the "adult in the room," deliberately contrasting his technocratic stoicism with what he terms the "aristocratic smugness" of Prime Minister Honda and the "populist theatrics" of OSP leader [[Rento Nakayama]]. He has frequently argued that the hung parliament produced a "Frankenstein government" incapable of coherent policy-making.

He has established a vigorous Shadow Cabinet, tasking Shadow Foreign Minister [[Reo Nakasone]] with holding the government accountable for the "En Crisis" and recent diplomatic blunders regarding the [[Ameka Islands]]. Domestically, Sadanuma has pledged to block any further tax increases proposed by the PPO-OSP coalition, arguing that the existing tax burden is already suffocating the middle class. He advocates for a "Zero-Based Budgeting" approach, demanding that every government program justify its existence anew rather than receiving automatic funding increases.

===The "Winter of Discontent" (2026)===
Sadanuma's leadership has faced its most significant test during the political turmoil of early 2026. With the "En Crisis" driving up the cost of living and the coalition government paralyzed by internal infighting over the FY2026 budget, Sadanuma has adopted an aggressive parliamentary strategy.

In February 2026, he famously declared the Honda administration "functionally brain-dead" during a fiery exchange in the Budget Committee. Capitalizing on the public rift between Prime Minister Honda and Welfare Minister Nakayama over the proposed "Inflation Relief Package," Sadanuma filed a formal motion of no confidence against the Cabinet. He argued that the government's inability to present a unified budget before the April deadline constituted a dereliction of duty.

Sadanuma has simultaneously courted the minor [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP), seeking to form a united opposition bloc that could block the budget and force a snap election. He has positioned the LDP as the only party capable of restoring fiscal sanity, warning that the OSP's demands for deficit spending would lead to a sovereign debt crisis similar to the one experienced in the 1990s.

===The "March Maelstrom" and Motion of No Confidence (2026)===
Following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] on March 1, 2026, Sadanuma pivoted the LDP's strategy from fiscal criticism to a broader indictment of the government's operational competence. He has characterized the tragedy not as an isolated accident, but as the "inevitable byproduct of a government that manages by spreadsheet rather than by sight."

====Tactical Response to the Rail Crisis====
While [[Rento Nakayama]] and the OSP focused on the human tragedy, Sadanuma utilized the crisis to attack the PPO’s administrative oversight. On March 5, he delivered a speech in the House of Commoners where he accused Prime Minister [[Shigetsuma Honda]] of "digital negligence," specifically citing the administration's mandate for drivers to use mobile devices for scheduling while in motion. 

Sadanuma's approach was carefully calibrated to appeal to both the outraged public and the business community. While he expressed sympathy for the victim, he focused his formal attacks on the "regulatory vacuum" created by the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet, arguing that the LDP would have ensured "traditional safety redundancy" alongside modernization. 

====Orchestrating the No-Confidence Vote====
As the [[Okaiken Transit Workers Union]] (OTWU) began its general strike on March 10, Sadanuma moved to capitalize on the chaos. He successfully pressured the [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP) to abandon their "Midnight Agreement" with Honda, effectively isolating the Prime Minister. 

On March 11, following the formal withdrawal of the OSP from the coalition, Sadanuma held a televised press conference where he confirmed the LDP would move a formal vote of no confidence against the Cabinet on March 12. "The era of the Frankenstein government is over," Sadanuma declared. "Okaiken cannot be governed by a Prime Minister who has lost his partner, his majority, and the trust of every citizen who steps onto a train platform."

====Campaign Positioning====
Ahead of the anticipated snap election, Sadanuma has rebranded the LDP under the slogan "Restoring the Standard of Safety." By positioning the LDP as a bridge between Honda’s "reckless modernization" and Nakayama’s "unfunded populism," he seeks to capture the center-ground voters who prioritize a return to order. However, political analysts note that Sadanuma faces a significant challenge in countering Nakayama's surging personal popularity, with the LDP leader frequently dismissing Nakayama's resignation as "theatrical abandonment" of his ministerial duties during a national emergency.

As of March 11, Sadanuma is reportedly finalizing his "Shadow Cabinet's First 100 Days" plan, which includes a total safety audit of [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR Harujima) and an emergency stabilization fund for the Sanese En.

==Political positions==
* Fiscal Policy: Sadanuma is a fiscal conservative. He opposes the scale of the National Universal Health Act, arguing it is financially unsustainable without broader structural reform. He supports reducing the corporate tax rate to stimulate investment while cutting administrative bloat.
* Foreign Policy: Through his Shadow Foreign Minister Reo Nakasone, Sadanuma advocates for a "Realist" foreign policy. He supports strengthening the alliance with [[Shayden]] and maintaining a robust defense posture, while criticizing the current government for sending "confusing signals" to international markets.
* Social Issues: Sadanuma is considered a social moderate. He has avoided the inflammatory rhetoric regarding immigration that characterized the LDP's right wing, focusing instead on the economic necessity of skilled labor, though he emphasizes strict border enforcement.

==Personal life==
Sadanuma is married to Yuko, a former classical pianist. They have three children. He is known for his serious, almost humorless public image, though colleagues describe him as a dry wit in private. His hobbies include marathon running and reading historical biographies.

